    historyContent: {
      origins: 'Five Card Draw evolved from older Persian (As-Nas) and French (Poque) games in the early 19th-century United States. It gained massive popularity during the American Civil War, becoming a staple of frontier life and eventually the standard for global homendgamers.',
      originCountries: [
        'USA',
      ],
      timeline: [
        '1830s: Poker develops on Mississippi riverboats.',
        "1860s: The 'Draw' rule is introduced, allowing players to improve their hands.",
        "1900s-1970s: The dominant form of Poker in the world before being overtaken by Seven Card Stud and eventually Texas Hold 'em.",
        "Present: Remains a popular 'gateway' game and a fundamental component of Mixed Games like HORSE.",
      ],
      evolution: "The introduction of the 'Jacks or Better' variant in the late 1800s helped prevent players from winning pots simply by betting blindly with garbage hands.",
      cultural: 'Famously depicted in classic Western cinema as the ultimate game of high-stakes bluffing and gunfights.',
