        rules: {
          objective: 'Win the pot by having the highest-ranking 5-card poker hand at showdown or by forcing all other players to fold.',
          gameplay: '1. The Deal: 5 cards dealt to each player.\n2. 1st Betting Round: Starts from the player to the left of the blinds/dealer.\n3. The Draw: Players discard 0-3 cards (or 4 if holding an Ace) and receive replacements.\n4. 2nd Betting Round: A final chance to bet or bluff based on the improved hand.\n5. Showdown: Remaining players reveal hands.',
          keyRules: [
            'Stand Pat: Choosing to draw zero cards, often signaling a very strong hand (like a Straight or Flush).',
            'Drawing 3: Standard for improving a single pair.',
            "Burning: The dealer usually 'burns' the top card before the draw to prevent cheating.",
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        strategy: {
          basic: "If your starting hand is less than a pair of Kings, you should usually fold early. Don't waste money 'chasing' a draw unless the pot odds are very favorable.",
          intermediate: "The 'One Card Draw' Bluff: If you draw only one card, opponents will assume you are fishing for a Straight or a Flush. You can use this to represent strength even if you have nothing.",
          advanced: 'Breaking a Pair: In high-level play, you might break a low pair to draw for a Straight Flush if the pot is massive, though this is statistically risky.',
          tips: [
            "Pay attention to how many cards opponents draw. A player drawing 3 usually has a pair; a player drawing 1 has a draw or two-pair; a player drawing 0 is 'standing pat' with a very strong hand.",
            "Position is everything. If you are the last to act, you have the most information about your opponents' drawing patterns.",
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
